{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "overall_rating_recommendation (Overall Rating and Recommended = yes/no)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "cabin_crew_attitude",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A candidate cause of the rating/recommendation. Crew attitude plausibly drives passenger satisfaction, so it is a treatment/driver being attributed, not a common cause of another treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "service_proactivity",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Another service-quality lever whose effect on the outcome is being attributed. It is a parallel driver of the rating, not a common cause of both a treatment and the outcome."
    },
    {
      "name": "ground_service_quality",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Check-in/boarding/connection problems are candidate causes of a poor rating; a driver/treatment, not an adjustment-set confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "ife_quality",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "In-flight entertainment quality is a candidate cause of satisfaction. Treatment/driver of the outcome."
    },
    {
      "name": "disruption_handling_quality",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "How disruptions are handled directly drives whether passengers recommend; a candidate cause, not a confounder of another cause."
    },
    {
      "name": "complaint_resolution_outcome",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Resolution outcome is a candidate cause of the recommendation decision. It sits on a driver path to the outcome, not a common cause of both a treatment and outcome."
    },
    {
      "name": "digital_system_failure",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A binary operational failure flag treated as a candidate cause of dissatisfaction; a driver/treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "value_for_money_perception",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "This nearly duplicates the existing 'Value For Money' rating dimension and is an evaluative judgement that lies ON the path from service quality to overall rating/recommendation; adjusting for it would be a mediator error, not a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "service_decline_perceived",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A summary perception (actual service quality -> perceived decline -> lower rating). It sits on the causal path and is close to the outcome, so it is a mediator rather than a common-cause confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "cabin_class_product_gap",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The gap between product and class expectations is an evaluative judgement directly upstream of (and nearly equivalent to) satisfaction; it is on the treatment->outcome path, i.e., a mediator, not a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "flight_duration_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A genuine contextual/pre-treatment variable derived from Route: flight length plausibly shapes the level and salience of service delivered (meals, IFE use, crew interaction) AND shapes expectations/ratings, so it can bias attribution if not adjusted. This is the only column functioning as a common-cause confounder, though it is closer to an effect modifier."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": [
    "cabin_crew_attitude",
    "service_proactivity",
    "ground_service_quality",
    "ife_quality",
    "disruption_handling_quality",
    "complaint_resolution_outcome",
    "digital_system_failure"
  ],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "flight_duration_category", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.15,
  "rationale": "The added columns richly supply the TREATMENT side of a causal attribution: they decompose the outcome into many candidate service-quality causes (crew attitude, proactivity, ground service, IFE, disruption/complaint handling, digital failures). However, the set is essentially a DRIVER DECOMPOSITION, not a confounder-adjusted causal design. Several columns (value_for_money_perception, service_decline_perceived, cabin_class_product_gap) are evaluative perceptions that lie ON the path to the rating — mediators/near-outcomes whose adjustment would bias, not clarify, attribution. Crucially, the operator surfaced almost no genuine CONFOUNDERS — variables that jointly cause a treatment AND the outcome and must be adjusted for. The one contextual variable, flight_duration_category, is not named in the query and is the only plausible common-cause/effect-modifier, and even it is borderline. Traveller type, class, and route (natural confounders) exist only in the original schema, not among the added columns. Thus the specific causal-attribution claim is only weakly met: treatment present = yes, but the confounder (adjustment) side is largely missing, so confounder_quality is low."
}

